"Every year, humanity throws away about two billion tonnes of trash. That's the World Bank's estimate, and it expects the number to climb to three point four billion tonnes a year by 2050.\n\nLandfills fill up. Some of it gets burned in the open. Some of it ends up in rivers and in the ocean.\n\nMeanwhile, about a hundred and fifty million kilometers away, there's a star that could swallow anything we throw at it.\n\nSo here's the obvious fix. Load the trash onto rockets, aim them at the Sun, and let it burn.\n\nIt sounds simple. But the Sun is one of the hardest places to reach in the whole solar system. And the reason has nothing to do with the heat.\n\nLet's start with where we are. Earth isn't sitting still. It's racing around the Sun at about thirty kilometers per second. That's roughly sixty-seven thousand miles per hour.\n\nAnd here's the part that's easy to miss. Everything on Earth is moving with it. You, your house, your trash, and every rocket sitting on a launch pad.\n\nSo when a rocket leaves Earth, it doesn't start from zero. It starts out already flying sideways around the Sun at thirty kilometers per second.\n\nThat's also why Earth doesn't fall into the Sun. In a way, it is falling, all the time. It's just moving sideways so fast that it keeps missing. That's what an orbit is.\n\nPicture yourself on a fast train. You drop a ball out of the window. It doesn't fall straight down next to you. It keeps the train's speed and flies forward with you.\n\nTrash launched from Earth does the same thing. It doesn't drop toward the Sun. It just keeps circling it, right alongside us.\n\nTo actually hit the Sun, a rocket has to cancel that sideways speed. Not speed up. Slow down. By a lot.\n\nSo how hard is that? NASA found out with the Parker Solar Probe.\n\nParker launched in 2018 to fly closer to the Sun than anything ever had. It didn't even need to hit the Sun. But NASA says it still had to get rid of about fifty-three thousand miles per hour of sideways speed.\n\nNASA puts it this way. Reaching the Sun takes fifty-five times more energy than reaching Mars.\n\nA powerful rocket wasn't enough on its own. So Parker borrowed help from Venus. It swung past Venus seven times, and every pass slowed it down a little more.\n\nIt took six years. On Christmas Eve 2024, Parker passed about three point eight million miles from the Sun's surface, moving at around four hundred and thirty thousand miles per hour. No spacecraft has ever come closer.\n\nAnd that leads to a strange fact. It's actually easier to throw something out of the solar system completely than to drop it into the Sun.\n\nStarting from Earth's orbit, escaping the Sun's pull takes about twelve extra kilometers per second. Falling straight into the Sun means taking away almost all of Earth's thirty.\n\nNow, let's add the trash.\n\nSay we only wanted to get one year of the world's trash into low Earth orbit. Not to the Sun. Just a few hundred kilometers up.\n\nSpaceX's Falcon Heavy is one of the most powerful rockets flying today. In its strongest setup, it can lift about sixty-four tonnes to low Earth orbit.\n\nTwo billion tonnes, sixty-four tonnes at a time, is about thirty-one million launches. For one year of trash. That's around eighty-six thousand launches every single day.\n\nFor comparison, the entire world attempted about two hundred and sixty orbital launches in 2024. That was a record.\n\nAnd remember, that's only to orbit. A rocket heading for the Sun could carry just a small part of that load.\n\nThen there's the rockets themselves. They're far more reliable than they used to be, but launches still fail sometimes.\n\nA rocket full of trash that breaks apart on the way up doesn't make the trash disappear. It spreads it across the sky, the sea, or the land below.\n\nThis isn't a new idea, either. In the 1970s and early 1980s, NASA studied launching one special kind of waste into space. Nuclear waste. It's small in volume and dangerous for a very long time, so if any trash was worth the effort, it was this.\n\nEven then, a 1973 study found that sending waste straight into the Sun wasn't possible with any of the rockets they considered. It simply took too much energy. They looked at pushing it out of the solar system instead.\n\nNASA's reports named the two biggest questions: safety and cost. And a 1981 report estimated that handling just eight tonnes of waste a year would take three Space Shuttle launches.\n\nIn the end, nuclear waste never went to space.\n\nSo why don't we just fire our trash into the Sun?\n\nBecause everything that leaves Earth is already flying sideways at thirty kilometers per second. Getting rid of that speed takes enormous amounts of energy, years of careful flying, and a launch about every second that never fails.\n\nThe real trash problem is down here. The World Bank estimates that a third or more of the world's waste isn't managed properly. It's dumped, or burned in the open.\n\nCollecting it, recycling it and burying it safely is a lot less exciting than a rocket to the Sun. But it's the fix that actually works.\n\nThe Sun looks close enough to aim at every single day. It's just a lot harder to hit than it looks.\n"
